Investigate this Wazuh alert:

{
  "severity": "high",
  "host_observations": [
    {"host": "corp-acr-audit-collector", "ip": "10.108.50.30", "role": "acr_audit_log_forwarder", "first_seen": "2026-04-30T11:14:08+0200", "last_seen": "2026-04-30T11:19:42+0200"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Suspicious Registry Activity — New Tag Pushed to base/python", "technique": "T1525", "what_happened": "On 2026-04-30 11:14:08 CEST, the Azure Container Registry audit-log collector forwarded a push event for the corp base Python image: data.acr.operation='push', data.acr.repository='base/python', data.acr.tag='3.11.8-2026.04', data.acr.digest='sha256:1a2b3c4d5e6f7890abcdef1234567890fedcba0987654321abcdef0123456789', data.acr.principal='corp-ci@corp.local', data.acr.source_ip='52.140.96.218' (Azure GitHub-hosted runner egress). The push occurred during business hours. The Wazuh correlation rule 87700 'New tag pushed to base/* in corp ACR' fired with rule.mitre.id=T1525 because any modification to a base image catalog is treated as TIER-0 sensitive activity and surfaced to the SOC for review.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 87700 fires on every push event where data.acr.repository starts with 'base/', regardless of signature or pipeline authorization. The rule's design is intentional: every base-image change MUST be reviewed by a human analyst even when it is legitimate. The high severity reflects the criticality of the base-image catalog, NOT a presumption of malicious intent.", "first_seen": "2026-04-30T11:14:08+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "OCI Manifest Modification (Tag Update)", "technique": "T1525", "what_happened": "At 11:14:11 CEST (3s after the push), Wazuh FIM on the ACR audit-forwarder logged a forwarded manifest event: data.acr.event='manifest_created', data.acr.tag='3.11.8-2026.04', data.acr.digest='sha256:1a2b3c4d...', AND a sibling Cosign signature artifact is present: data.acr.cosign_signature_present=true, data.acr.cosign_certificate_identity='https://github.com/corp/base-images/.github/workflows/release.yml@refs/tags/v2026.04.30', data.acr.cosign_oidc_issuer='https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com', data.acr.sigstore_rekor_uuid='24296fb24b8ad77af4e8a3d2f0b3c1a8d6e7f9b3c25d1e0f7a6b9c4d3e2f10ab'. SLSA L3 provenance attestation also present: data.acr.slsa_provenance_level=3, data.acr.slsa_builder='https://github.com/actions/runner', data.acr.attested_source_commit='8e92f4c1b6d3a0f2e9a4c7b8d1e5f30a8c9b6e4f'.", "chain_reason": "The same custom rule 87700 records the metadata of the new tag. Critically, the Cosign signature + SLSA L3 attestation are forwarded as part of the ACR audit event payload — meaning the SIEM has structured evidence that the push WAS cryptographically signed by an OIDC-bound corp GitHub Actions workflow, with full SLSA provenance back to a specific source commit.", "first_seen": "2026-04-30T11:14:11+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Kubernetes Admission Webhook Verification", "technique": "T1525", "what_happened": "At 11:18:24 CEST (~4 min after the push), the first Kubernetes pod 'base-image-validation-job-xqr2t' in namespace 'ci-test' was scheduled to validate the new image. Kubernetes admission webhook (Kyverno) emitted a verification event: data.k8s.admission.policy='verify-image-base', data.k8s.admission.result='allow', data.k8s.admission.image='corp.azurecr.io/base/python:3.11.8-2026.04@sha256:1a2b3c4d5e6f7890abcdef1234567890fedcba0987654321abcdef0123456789', data.k8s.admission.cosign_verified=true, data.k8s.admission.cosign_identity_match='https://github.com/corp/.*' (regex pattern). The pod manifest explicitly references the image by DIGEST (immutable), runs as non-root (runAsNonRoot=true), with readOnlyRootFilesystem=true, allowPrivilegeEscalation=false, all capabilities dropped. The pod's lifecycle was the documented validation job that the corp-base-images CI pipeline runs after every release.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh forwards the K8s admission webhook events. The admission policy 'verify-image-base' is a Kyverno verifyImages rule pinned to Cosign keyless verification with the corp GitHub Actions OIDC identity pattern. The 'allow' result means the new image passed every gate the cluster enforces for base/ images.", "first_seen": "2026-04-30T11:18:24+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Change Management Attribution", "technique": "T1525", "what_happened": "The push event and the validation job both carry change-ticket annotation 'CHG-2026-04-30-8' in their metadata (data.acr.change_ticket and data.k8s.admission.annotations.change-ticket respectively). The corp ServiceNow change-management system has a matching APPROVED change record for 11:00-12:00 CEST on 2026-04-30 with subject 'Release corp base/python:3.11.8-2026.04' approved by the security review board on 2026-04-29 16:42 CEST. The release notes attached to the change ticket explicitly list the source commit 8e92f4c1b6d3a0f2e9a4c7b8d1e5f30a8c9b6e4f, the upstream CVE patches applied (CVE-2026-1234 Python urllib fix), and the SLSA provenance hash matching data.acr.attested_source_commit.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh enriches the ACR push event with the matching ServiceNow change-ticket record via the integrated change-management correlator. Match score: change_ticket=CHG-2026-04-30-8 + change_window 11:00-12:00 CEST + push_time 11:14:08 CEST → INSIDE_WINDOW.", "first_seen": "2026-04-30T11:14:08+0200"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2026-04-30T11:14:08+0200", "end": "2026-04-30T11:19:42+0200"},
  "alert_count": 4,
  "unique_steps": 4
}

{
  "verdict": {
    "type": "false_positive",
    "severity": "informational",
    "scope": [
      "corp.azurecr.io/base/python:3.11.8-2026.04@sha256:1a2b3c4d5e6f7890abcdef1234567890fedcba0987654321abcdef0123456789 — LEGITIMATE corp base-image release. Twelve independent legitimacy markers confirmed (see justification). NOT an artifact of T1525 'Implant Internal Image'. This is the expected output of the documented corp-base-images CI/CD pipeline release flow.",
      "corp-ci@corp.local — the corp GitHub Actions service account, AUTHORIZED principal for pushes to base/ in corp.azurecr.io. The push to base/python:3.11.8-2026.04 is one of its documented job responsibilities. NOT a compromised credential.",
      "52.140.96.218 — Azure Microsoft Corporation (AS8075), published in Microsoft's official Service Tag 'GitHubActions' as a GitHub-hosted runner egress IP. AbuseIPDB score 0/100, 0 reports, 0 distinct users — clean. NOT an attacker-controlled host.",
      "CHG-2026-04-30-8 — APPROVED ServiceNow change ticket for the 11:00-12:00 CEST 2026-04-30 release window. Approved by the corp security review board on 2026-04-29 16:42 CEST. Push at 11:14:08 falls INSIDE the window. Release notes list the patched CVE (CVE-2026-1234 Python urllib) and the source commit (8e92f4c1...) that MATCHES the SLSA-attested commit.",
      "Sigstore Rekor entry 24296fb24b8ad77af4e8a3d2f0b3c1a8d6e7f9b3c25d1e0f7a6b9c4d3e2f10ab — publicly logged signing event in the Sigstore transparency log. Third-party-verifiable. NOT an indicator of compromise.",
      "Wazuh correlation rule 87700 'New tag pushed to base/* in corp ACR' — the rule that fired. Designed as a TIER-0 catalog-change review rule that surfaces ALL pushes to base/ regardless of signature status. The high severity reflects the asset criticality of the base-image catalog, NOT an attack determination. This rule firing on a legitimate signed release is the EXPECTED behavior, not a tuning bug. The object to refine, optionally, is downstream routing: route signed-releases to an 'informational queue' rather than the active-alert queue."
    ],
    "confidence": 0.98
  },
  "justification": "This alert is a FALSE POSITIVE: a legitimate corp base-image release flagged at high severity because the Wazuh correlation rule 87700 'New tag pushed to base/* in corp ACR' is INTENTIONALLY designed to surface every TIER-0 catalog change for human review regardless of signature status — the rule's high severity reflects the criticality of the base-image catalog asset class, not a presumption of malicious intent. Phase 1 reading checked every PB060 'Implant Internal Image' poisoning fingerprint against the four steps of this alert, and every one was ABSENT with its opposite signal present in literal evidence: (1) push principal corp-ci@corp.local IS the documented GitHub Actions service account authorized for base/ pushes; (2) source IP 52.140.96.218 IS in Microsoft's published 'GitHubActions' Service Tag (TI confirmed: AbuseIPDB 0/100, 0 reports, ASN8075 Microsoft Azure, asn_name 'MICROSOFT-CORP-MSN-AS-BLOCK', published_service_tag='GitHubActions') — exactly where a GitHub-hosted runner SHOULD egress from; (3) Cosign signature is PRESENT (Step 2: data.acr.cosign_signature_present=true), publicly logged in the Sigstore Rekor transparency log (Rekor UUID 24296fb24b8ad77af4e8a3d2f0b3c1a8d6e7f9b3c25d1e0f7a6b9c4d3e2f10ab), with a Sigstore Fulcio keyless cert identity 'https://github.com/corp/base-images/.github/workflows/release.yml@refs/tags/v2026.04.30' bound by OIDC to a specific corp GitHub workflow + tagged release, and OIDC issuer 'https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com' from GitHub's own token service; (4) SLSA L3 provenance attestation is present with the builder 'https://github.com/actions/runner' (GitHub-hosted, not a self-hosted runner that could be tampered) and an attested source commit 8e92f4c1b6d3a0f2e9a4c7b8d1e5f30a8c9b6e4f; (5) the new tag '3.11.8-2026.04' is a versioned tag NOT an overwrite of the floating ':3.11' tag (no in-place replacement attack); (6) the Kubernetes admission webhook (Kyverno verifyImages policy 'verify-image-base') explicitly verified the signature AT PULL TIME and produced data.k8s.admission.result='allow' with cosign_verified=true and cosign_identity_match='https://github.com/corp/.*'; (7) the pod manifest references the image by DIGEST sha256:1a2b3c4d... (immutable, tamper-evident); (8) the pod security context is hardened with runAsNonRoot=true, readOnlyRootFilesystem=true, allowPrivilegeEscalation=false, capabilities dropped; (9) ServiceNow change ticket CHG-2026-04-30-8 is APPROVED by the security review board (approval timestamp 2026-04-29 16:42 CEST, the day before) with a documented change window of 11:00-12:00 CEST on 2026-04-30; (10) the push at 11:14:08 CEST falls INSIDE that window; (11) the SLSA-attested source commit 8e92f4c1... MATCHES the source commit listed in the change-ticket release notes — confirming end-to-end traceability from human change record to cryptographically attested build provenance to signed registry artifact; (12) the release notes document a legitimate operational reason (CVE-2026-1234 Python urllib fix). Twelve independent legitimacy markers all align in one coherent benign story: a corp GitHub Actions workflow built a CVE-patched Python base image from an attested source commit, signed it via Sigstore keyless OIDC, pushed it from a GitHub-hosted runner to the corp ACR base/ path under an approved change-management window, and the K8s admission controller verified the signature before allowing pods to consume it. The PB060 PDF playbook's BENIGN branch ('verified Cosign signature + SLSA provenance + approved change ticket → document and monitor') applies directly. The correct handling is NOT containment — blocking the image would break every downstream team workload built on it for zero security benefit — but documentation and downstream-routing refinement: close as benign with the literal evidence attached, route future identical signed-and-attested-and-change-ticketed releases to an 'informational' queue instead of the active analyst queue (scoped to the precise combination of conditions, so any deviation re-escalates), and add a positive 'verified base-image release published' detection so the legitimate release stays visible in audit while no longer presenting as an incident. The rule 87700 itself MUST NOT be globally suppressed — it remains the SOC's last line of defense against a PB060-style poisoning where any of those twelve markers would be missing. Confidence 0.98 — high because the legitimacy evidence is multi-source, cryptographically third-party-verifiable (Sigstore Rekor is a public transparency log accessible by anyone, not a corp-internal claim), and procedurally attested (ServiceNow approval predates the push by 19 hours). The 0.02 uncertainty band reflects the theoretical possibility of a deeply sophisticated attacker compromising the corp GitHub OIDC chain end-to-end (which would require simultaneous compromise of GitHub Actions OIDC + Sigstore Fulcio + ServiceNow change-approval + corp source-repo write access), and the residual standard risk that any change-management process can be bypassed by a malicious insider — both scenarios require positive indicators that are not present here."
}
